Note - There are a lot of MNCs which use Terraform community version even today.

Hey, in the current organization, we have evaluated Terraform Enterprise and Community version. We are definitely aware within the Terraform Enterprise version 
there are features like drift detection. There are features like state file management, where state file is handled by the Terraform enterprise, and also Rbac.
That is proper security.

Who can access the Terraform state file and who can access the Terraform enterprise?
We have a very strong DevOps team. We actually have a team of 10 to 11 DevOps instance (Or you can also say we have a short DevOps team, but strong DevOps team.)
And we have decided to implement these features on our own. So we made sure that the Terraform state file is stored on a remote backend.

So currently we are using S3 bucket. And within the S3 bucket we have implemented we have enabled versioning. We have enabled locking and enabled security.
So this way our state file has backups. Our state file is also logged on concurrent executions, and our state file is also secure. 
And then we have implemented a very strong Rbac. So we store the Terraform CL files on a git repository with strict restrictions.
So all our CL files and even if someone wants to execute the Terraform ci CD, they need access to the git repository.

And finally, when it comes to drift detection, which is another feature offered by Terraform enterprise, we made sure we implement zero privilege or least 
privilege on the cloud provider. So no developer can actually go to the AWS or Azure Cloud Platform and modify the resources on their own if they have to 
modify the resources. They actually have to head to Terraform and they have to update the resources using Terraform. 
Even if they want to delete the resources, they have to head head to terraform and they have to delete the resources from Terraform.

Short Explanation:
In a nutshell, Terraform is our single source of truth or single point of contact for creating the infrastructure. No developer can manually update 
the infrastructure on the cloud provider or delete the infrastructure on the cloud provider. So there is no point of drift detection. We have handled it.
So this way we decided to go with community version of Terraform instead of Terraform enterprise version and save a lot of money for our organization.

Only things that Terraform enterprise offer better than community version is drift detection and proper state file management with Rbac.
